<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Lilybaeum</h1><p class="st-meta">397 BCE – 241 BCE (approx.) · capital Lilybaeum</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Carthaginian fortress city at Sicily's western tip, founded after the fall of Motya, which withstood Pyrrhus and a nine-year Roman siege.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysius-i-of-syracuse">Dionysius I</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/motya">Motya</a> in 397 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> settled the survivors on Cape Lilybaeum, the westernmost point of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. The new city, on a headland with a sheltered harbour facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, became Carthage's main base on the island.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Lilybaeum was a heavily fortified port with walls, towers and a deep ditch. Its population was mainly Punic with Greek elements, and its trade linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. It successfully resisted Pyrrhus in 276 BCE and, in the First Punic War, a Roman siege by land and sea from 250 to 241 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> ceded Lilybaeum to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by the treaty ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a> in 241 BCE. It became the seat of one of the quaestors of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, a post <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> held in 75 BCE. It later became the Arab port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marsala">Marsa Allah</a>, modern Marsala.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lilybaeum.jpg" alt="Lilybaeum" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Fedina31 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marsala,_Sicily._Saline.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>397 BCE – 241 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lilybaeum</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Punic religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Punic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lilybaion, Marsala (ancient)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-319&amp;focus=lilybaeum" aria-label="Lilybaeum on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4990 818 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lilybaeum lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5310 1074L5311 1072M5310 1074L5259 1106M5310 1074L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5311 1072h0M5259 1106h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5310 1074h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5310 1074h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-319&amp;focus=lilybaeum">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
